    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"Water turbidity has a major impact on the viability of various marine communities in Florida Bay such as seagrasses and hardbottoms. Resuspension events can lead to damage to, or even burial of, many of these bottom communities. Such events may also lead to the introduction of nutrients into the water column, causing algal blooms. Current monitoring programs provide comprehensive data on water quality in monthly or quarterly intervals. However, these programs cannot address the questions of the frequency and magnitude of turbidity events, and the quantity of sediment being moved. For this project, we are using remotely sensed AVHRR data to examine the spatial and temporal patterns of water turbidity. This information is necessary as part of the efforts to evaluate environmental stress throughout Florida Bay and Biscayne Bay.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
